Make the TACIT DSC branch predictor an elaboration-time option, default OFF.

WHAT IT IS.  TacitEncoder instantiates a DSCBranchPredictor: 1024 two-bit
saturating counters in a Chisel register Vec, read through a dynamically indexed
1024:1 mux and written through a 1024-way decoder.  On a 7-series FPGA that is
the classic register-file-in-flops pattern.  Measured post-route in the shipped
PYNQ-Z1 bitstreams (build_rocket_mic_z1/reports/post_route_util_hier.rpt):

    .../applyOrElse/bp   DSCBranchPredictor_77   3045 LUT   2048 FF   (hart 0)
    .../applyOrElse/bp   DSCBranchPredictor      3037 LUT   2048 FF   (hart 1)

which is 6,082 of the TACIT block's 8,132 LUT -- 74.8% of it, and 83% of each
encoder's flip-flops.

WHY IT CAN GO.  The predictor's output reaches nothing in any trace this repo
has ever taken, because every consumer of `bp.io.resp` is gated on
`is_bp_mode = (io.control.bp_mode === 2.U)` and:

  * `trace_bp_mode` in TraceEncoderController.scala is `RegInit(0.U(32.W))`, so
    the encoder comes up in BrTarget mode (0).  That is what the generated
    Verilog we ship says too: `trace_bp_mode <= 32'h0`.
  * nothing in this repo ever writes offset 0x24 -- no sample, no script, no
    patch, and the Zephyr header only declares the field.
  * the decoder's `--br-mode` defaults to 0 (BrTarget) and no lab passes it, so
    src/frontend/bp_double_saturating_counter.rs is never stepped either.

So hardware and decoder have both been in BrTarget the whole time, and every
validated number -- 534,990 instructions at 2.021 bits/instruction, the boot
trace, the dual-hart skew -- was produced with the predictor sitting idle.
This is dead-logic removal, not a compression trade.

WHAT THE PATCH DOES.

  DSCBranchPredictor.scala   TacitBPParams gains `enable: Boolean = true`.  The
      default keeps every existing call site byte-identical in behaviour.

  TacitEncoder.scala         `bp` becomes an Option, so with enable = false the
      module is never instantiated -- the counters, the read mux and the write
      decoder are not emitted rather than emitted and left unread.  `is_bp_mode`
      becomes the Scala literal `false.B`, which folds away bp_hit_count,
      bp_miss_flag, bp_flush_hit and bp_hit_packet with it, and `is_bt_mode`
      becomes `true.B`, so the bp_mode register is IGNORED rather than able to
      select a mode this encoder can no longer implement.  That last point is
      what makes the decode side need no flag: a trace from a no-predictor
      encoder is always a BrTarget trace, which is the decoder's own default.

  TacitConfigs.scala         `WithTacitEncoder(useBP: Boolean = false)`.  Our
      configs get the default.  Pass `true` for BrPredict (and then pass
      `--br-mode 2` to the decoder).

WHAT IT DOES NOT DO.  The 0x24 bp_mode register still exists, still resets to 0
and is still readable and writable -- only its output to the encoder is gone.
The generated DTS and both register maps are unchanged, so Zephyr's tacit.h and
every lab script are untouched.

MEASURED RESULT.  Re-elaborating PynqZ2RocketTacitConfig against this patch:
DSCBranchPredictor.sv is no longer emitted, and of the 405 generated files the
previous bundle and this one have in common, 396 are byte-identical, 5 differ
only in `// @[...]` source annotations (line shifts from the comments above),
and 4 differ for real: TacitEncoder.sv, RocketTile.sv and
TraceEncoderController.sv (all three lose the bp_mode connection), plus one
simulation-only `$error` string in Rocket.sv whose embedded RocketCore.scala
line number moved for an unrelated reason -- see TACIT_AREA.md.

APPLIED BY scripts/03_patch_tacit.sh, not by hand: $CHIPYARD_DIR is a shared
checkout.  `generators/tacit` is UNTRACKED in the donor tree, so this patch
cannot be verified by scripts/02_verify_patches.sh the way the others are; the
apply script's --check reverse-applies it instead.  The one file this change
touches that IS tracked lives in patches/0031.

